# SYNOPSIS

```text
driver NAME
class CLASS
match KIND ID
[directive ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

Each driver's directory holds a `driver.manifest`, which tells devmgr(8) which devices the driver serves.
The build collects every one into bootfs's `etc/drivers`, and devmgr matches each device it finds against them as data.

A driver's manifest is directives, one a line; `#` and what follows it are a comment.
Several drivers may follow one another in one file, each starting at its `driver` line.

: `driver`
  The driver's program: bootfs's `bin/<name>`.
  It comes first.
: `class`
  Its function class, such as `input` or `audio`.
: `match`
  A rule the driver matches devices by.
  The kinds are `pci <vendor>:<device>`, `pci-class <cc>.<ss>` or `<cc>.<ss>.<pp>` with a programming interface, `acpi <hid>`, `acpi-compatible <cid>` and `dt <compatible>`, a devicetree node's `compatible` (`dt spacemit,k1-emac`), in hexadecimal where they are numbers.
  A driver needs one at least.
: `fallback`
  A generic driver: any other driver's match wins over its.
: `dma-bits`
  How many bits of address its device can reach by DMA, 12 to 64.
: `sd`
  A block driver's sd(3) letter, a capital: devmgr binds its disks at `/dev/sd`<letter>`0`, `1`, and so on.
: `poweroff`
  Its `ctl` takes `poweroff`, which turns the machine off, as a PMIC's driver can where the firmware can't: devmgr sends `/dev/reboot`'s `poweroff` there before asking the firmware (reboot(8)).
: `coherence`
  `coherence coherent|noncoherent SOURCE...`: whether its device's DMA is cache coherent, as observed, and where it was observed (a document, a board run, a vendor's driver read as documentation).
  It wins over the device's `_CCA`, and devmgr's `status` gives the source with the answer.
  A driver whose record says `noncoherent` is started with `--noncoherent`.
: `own-mmu`
  Its device translates through page tables its driver writes, so no IOMMU confines it whatever the firmware's tables name.
  devmgr gives it the BTI of a device behind no IOMMU, and the kernel warns, as it does for that one, that the device's DMA reaches physical memory.
  `vx hardware trust` lists the driver in its board's trusted base (vx-hardware(1)).
: `power-up`
  `power-up graph`: devmgr turns its device on before it starts the driver, in the order its links give: the SCMI power domains of `_DSD`'s `power-domains`, then its clocks (`CLKT`), then its power resources (`_PR0` and `_DSD`'s `power-supply`), then its resets released (`RSTL`).
  Each step goes through what owns it: the SCMI agent (scmi(3)), devmgr's own AML, the reset controller's driver (sky1-reset(3)).
  The driver starts with its device on, and `power DEVICE off` in devmgr(8)'s `ctl` cuts the same steps in the reverse order.
  A device whose record has no such line is left as firmware left it, and its driver turns on what it needs.
: `power-clocks`
  `power-clocks NAME...`: of the device's clocks, turn on only these, by the names its `CLKT` gives; all of them where the record has none.
: `reset-pulse`
  `reset-pulse MICROSECONDS`: pulse each of the device's resets, 1 to 100000: asserted for that long, released, and given as long again, as a block that needs a reset and not only its release does; where the record has none, a reset is released.

devmgr matches in levels: exact ids first (a PCI vendor and device, an ACPI hardware id), then compatible ids, ACPI's or a devicetree node's `compatible`, in the order the device lists them, then classes, a programming interface before none, then the fallback drivers' rules in the same order.
The first level any driver matches at decides.
Two drivers matching at it is an error, and neither runs.

# EXAMPLES

```text
driver virtio-input
class input
match pci 1af4:1052
```

# FILES

: `drivers/**/driver.manifest`
  In the tree, each driver's.
: `etc/drivers`
  In bootfs, all of them, in path order.

